    "body": "## What\n\n`needsNonInvokerSigningBy()` now walks CAP-71 delegate trees. Along with the top-level addresses, it lists the unsigned delegates under a `C…` account, including nested ones, whether or not the account itself has signed. There is no opt-in flag: a caller asking who still needs to sign gets the delegates too.\n\n`signAuthEntries()` still selects and signs top-level addresses only, and its pre-flight check still uses the top-level list. When the `address` it is given is only an unsigned delegate, it throws `NoSignatureNeeded` naming the address and pointing to `authorizeEntry` with `forAddress`, instead of reporting that nothing is left to sign.\n\n`sign()` and `signAndSend()` run the check with delegates included, so they now also reject an unsigned `G…` delegate. They still skip `C…` addresses. Both take `ignoreContractDelegates` (default `false`), which skips the check for delegates under a contract account. `G…` addresses are still checked.\n\nThe walk lives in `pendingSigners` (`src/contract/utils.ts`), which reuses `signaturePresent` from `src/base/auth.ts`. The contract auth guide section from #1745 is updated to match.\n\nCloses #1655.\n\n## Why\n\nThe method looked only at the top-level node, so a delegate that never signed was never reported (#1655). Listing delegates by default means the address a caller has to chase down actually appears in the list, and the error from `sign()` that points at `needsNonInvokerSigningBy` leads somewhere useful.\n\nThe walk reports what the signatures show and does not guess at account policy:\n\n- A `G…` account needs its own signature, and its delegates are ignored. The host's built-in account check reads only the account's own signature list; delegates are reached only through `delegate_account_auth`, which only a contract's `__check_auth` calls. CAP-72 would change this, so the rule is marked to revisit.\n- An unsigned `C…` account stays listed even after its delegates have signed. CAP-71 allows both a delegates-only account (top-level `Void`) and an owner-plus-delegates account, and the entry looks the same either way. Dropping the account would let `sign()` pass a transaction the network rejects. Keeping it listed means a delegates-only caller filters out an address it already knows. The guide says to re-simulate after signing, since enforcing-mode simulation runs `__check_auth`.\n- A signed `C…` account's delegates are still walked. Its `__check_auth` may check its own signature and also call `delegate_account_auth`, so an unsigned delegate under it is reported. Simulation never returns the delegates variant, so an unsigned delegate the client attached almost always means someone still has to sign. The list can still include delegates a subset-using `__check_auth` never reads; pass `ignoreContractDelegates` to `sign()` for such accounts.\n",
